{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPYr5WanPVMVtNLP8hNQ5y","type":"tool_result","content":"intake: snapshot: 2 registry file(s) copied to docs/archive/last_approved (RE-ATTESTED: LLR-208, LLR-307, LLR-337, TC-206, TC-325, TC-367 — recorded in the snapshot's stamp)\r\nEXIT 0\n M docs/archive/last_approved/README.md\n M docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml\n M docs/archive/last_approved/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\n M docs/archive/last_approved/docs/test/test-cases.toml","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73ae5cf9-d335-4bb7-a08f-7631da597f26","uuid":"949a60f7-ba76-4f29-986e-0b865320e932","timestamp":"2026-10-19T21:51:51.452Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"intake: snapshot: 2 registry file(s) copied to docs/archive/last_approved (RE-ATTESTED: LLR-208, LLR-307, LLR-337, TC-206, TC-325, TC-367 — recorded in the snapshot's stamp)\r\nEXIT 0\n M docs/archive/last_approved/README.md\n M docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml\n M docs/archive/last_approved/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\n M docs/archive/last_approved/docs/test/test-cases.toml","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-925\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":156,"oldLines":3,"newStart":156,"newLines":4,"lines":[" - 2026-10-18 — refresh under approval. Copied: system-requirements.toml (re-attested: SR-209); low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-248). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."," - 2026-10-18 — refresh under approval. Copied: test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-242). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."," - 2026-10-19 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-248); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-242). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes.","+- 2026-10-19 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-208, LLR-307, LLR-337); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-206, TC-325, TC-367). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-925\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\acts.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":816,"oldLines":3,"newStart":816,"newLines":9,"lines":[" date = \"2026-10-19\""," approved = []"," reattested = [\"LLR-248\", \"TC-242\"]","+","+[[act]]","+seq = 135","+date = \"2026-10-19\"","+approved = []","+reattested = [\"LLR-208\", \"LLR-307\", \"LLR-337\", \"TC-206\", \"TC-325\", \"TC-367\"]"]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-925\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\requirements\\low-level-requirements.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":1438,"oldLines":7,"newStart":1438,"newLines":7,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-156\"]"," title = \"Link-aware spec-move ritual (move + relink indivisible)\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/spec_move.py\"","-code_symbol = \"move_spec/expected_relink/expected_rebase/archive_dest\"","+code_symbol = \"move_spec/expected_relink/exact_relink/expected_rebase/archive_dest\""," detail = \"\"\"move_spec moves a registry/spec file and keeps every markdown link in the repo true as ONE indivisible operation: the moved file's own relative targets are re-relativised against its new directory, every inbound link whose target resolves to the vacated path is redirected (link text untouched, #fragments carried, external/protocol-relative/bare-anchor targets left alone, root-relative targets excused only in the rebase half, line endings preserved via newline=''), and every rewritten file is staged with the move so the ritual lands as one changeset. integrate.claim and handback.close_partial call it for the claim and close moves; the CLI performs the worker's terminal close move and the dated docs/archive/specs/ archival. expected_relink is the pure oracle integrate._abandoned_claim compares byte-for-byte to excuse a crashed claim's own relink writes without widening its conviction to any .md edit.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"A bare move strands both link directions and the break surfaces late, on the composed tree (measured: one probe archival took the broken-link count from 4 to 8 - 3 outbound, 1 inbound). Making move+relink one indivisible operation is a constraint that leaves the two-thirds state unrepresentable, rather than a check that detects it after the parallel work is done.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-139)\""]},{"oldStart":2197,"oldLines":7,"newStart":2197,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"The per-review-scope verdict rollup: generated, checked, never an input\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/gen_verdict_rollup.py\""," code_symbol = \"scopes/render/targets/_extra/_off_trunk_refusal/main\"","-detail = \"\"\"One file per review scope under docs/reviews/rollup/<train>.md, compiled from the round files that scope carries: ordinal, phase, reviewed sha, parsed verdict word, finding count and filename, one row each. The scope SET is the train field the round-file name parser itself returns, taken over every round file found under docs/reviews/ - not the directory layout, because a second notion of what a review scope is can disagree with the first: enumerating directories skipped the flat pre-train layout that parser supports, which produced an empty rollup directory AND a --check calling it fresh against the same empty target set. That layout has no directory to be named after, so its rollup takes a reserved stem, and the one name collision that leaves is REFUSED by name on both the check and the write arm rather than resolved by a last write - two scopes sharing one output file leave the other permanently stale. The scope is the TRAIN and not the work item, because a traincar is one review scope - a worker schedules its round only once every assigned item is built and the round covers the combined train diff, so a per-item rollup would have to invent a slice of a round that does not exist. Declared in the toolchain file's generated section, freshness-gated by --check at the commit floor, and standing down on a work branch with the rest of the trunk-owned artifacts - the one member of that set whose SOURCE a branch necessarily writes, since the round files are a lane's own committed evidence. THE WRITER THAT CLOSES THAT CIRCLE IS THE TRUNK STEP'S REGEN SET, named by the id it carries there: the `verdict-rollup` row, armed by the presence of docs/reviews/ and skipped with a printed notice wherever that directory is absent, a LEAF whose position in the dependency order is therefore free. TWO mechanisms hold the exclusive-writer clause above and they hold DIFFERENT halves of it, so neither substitutes for the other. What ENFORCES it is `_off_trunk_refusal`: a direct write is refused with exit 2 whenever the checkout sits on a branch other than the trunk (agent_common.trunk_name, the PRIMARY checkout's branch, so a single-checkout repo is its own trunk and an attended run is unaffected), with exactly one caller exempt - the trunk step's own `--trunk-step`, because the station's refresh runs the trunk step INSIDE the lane worktree, where the branch is a work branch and the writer is still the trunk step. --check never writes and is never refused. The refusal is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces: without it the generator returned 0 and wrote the directory in a claimed lane, and the work-branch freshness stand-down then HID the write, which is the one failure this file's stand-down cannot report. What keeps the artifact FRESH is membership in that regen set, which is part of THIS contract and not an implementation accident: a work branch stands the freshness check down and commits the round files, so a trunk step that does not run this generator leaves the artifact written by nobody, and the first commit floor that reads it reds on a staleness no lane is permitted to clear. The generator OWNS that directory rather than only writing into it: --check reports an absent file, a diverged one and an EXTRA one - a retired review scope keeping its rollup is the same lie as an out-of-date one - on normalized line endings, and the WRITE path prunes the extra in the pass that writes the rest. Both halves or neither: reporting a file the regenerator would never touch made the remedy the failure message names unable to clear the failure, which on a step sitting at the commit floor and in the trunk freshness set is an unbreakable red under a misleading instruction. It names NO governing verdict: governing is tree identity, a question about a branch's current tree that can only be answered against a repository at a moment, so every row here is a fact about a file.\"\"\"","+detail = \"\"\"One file per review scope under docs/reviews/rollup/<train>.md, compiled from the round files that scope carries: ordinal, phase, reviewed sha, parsed verdict word, finding count and filename, one row each. The scope SET is the train field the round-file name parser itself returns, taken over every round file found under docs/reviews/ - not the directory layout, because a second notion of what a review scope is can disagree with the first: enumerating directories skipped the flat pre-train layout that parser supports, which produced an empty rollup directory AND a --check calling it fresh against the same empty target set. That layout has no directory to be named after, so its rollup takes a reserved stem, and the one name collision that leaves is REFUSED by name on both the check and the write arm rather than resolved by a last write - two scopes sharing one output file leave the other permanently stale. The scope is the TRAIN and not the work item, because a traincar is one review scope - a worker schedules its round only once every assigned item is built and the round covers the combined train diff, so a per-item rollup would have to invent a slice of a round that does not exist. Declared in the toolchain file's generated section, freshness-gated by --check at the commit floor, and standing down on a work branch with the rest of the trunk-owned artifacts - the one member of that set whose SOURCE a branch necessarily writes, since the round files are a lane's own committed evidence. THE WRITER THAT CLOSES THAT CIRCLE IS THE TRUNK STEP'S REGEN SET, named by the id it carries there: the `verdict-rollup` row, armed by the presence of docs/reviews/ and skipped with a printed notice wherever that directory is absent, a LEAF whose position in the dependency order is therefore free. TWO mechanisms hold the exclusive-writer clause above and they hold DIFFERENT halves of it, so neither substitutes for the other. What ENFORCES it is `_off_trunk_refusal`: a direct write is refused with exit 2 whenever the checkout sits on a branch other than the trunk (the declared trunk (agent_common.trunk_name), in any worktree; with the trunk undeclared, the write is refused naming the declaration), with exactly one caller exempt - the trunk step's own `--trunk-step`, because the station's refresh runs the trunk step INSIDE the lane worktree, where the branch is a work branch and the writer is still the trunk step. --check never writes and is never refused. The refusal is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces: without it the generator returned 0 and wrote the directory in a claimed lane, and the work-branch freshness stand-down then HID the write, which is the one failure this file's stand-down cannot report. What keeps the artifact FRESH is membership in that regen set, which is part of THIS contract and not an implementation accident: a work branch stands the freshness check down and commits the round files, so a trunk step that does not run this generator leaves the artifact written by nobody, and the first commit floor that reads it reds on a staleness no lane is permitted to clear. The generator OWNS that directory rather than only writing into it: --check reports an absent file, a diverged one and an EXTRA one - a retired review scope keeping its rollup is the same lie as an out-of-date one - on normalized line endings, and the WRITE path prunes the extra in the pass that writes the rest. Both halves or neither: reporting a file the regenerator would never touch made the remedy the failure message names unable to clear the failure, which on a step sitting at the commit floor and in the trunk freshness set is an unbreakable red under a misleading instruction. It names NO governing verdict: governing is tree identity, a question about a branch's current tree that can only be answered against a repository at a moment, so every row here is a fact about a file.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"The hand-authored per-item rollup was the merge gate's only input and nothing in the kit wrote it, so every mechanized lane stopped at a human - and because it paraphrased evidence that already existed, it was also the one artifact in the tree that was neither generated, checked nor declared while the doctrine everywhere else refuses that shape. Splitting the roles is what makes both halves honest: the gate reads the evidence, and the summary a human genuinely wants to read becomes derived state that cannot drift from it. Writing a governing verdict into the file would rebuild the retired artifact under a new name - a checked-in summary that reads as authority and is stale the moment the branch moves - so the file states facts about files and stops.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"TC-206\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3258,"oldLines":8,"newStart":3258,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-156\"]"," title = \"A Done-when blessing binds the exact changed checklist\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/done_when.py\"","-code_symbol = \"bound_items/digest/covering/_copy_at/claim_copy/unreadable_reason/blessing/blessing_owed/hold_reason/spec_at/_wi_hold/lane_hold/staged_closes/classify_delta/staged_changes/_staged_base/staged_hold\"","-detail = \"bound_items reads each current Done-when item that only ticks or evidences a claim-time item as that claim-time item; digest binds the work-item id and those items as sha256:<16 hex>. covering reads verdict lines under review records only through the one accepted-verdict reader: only a bound verdict whose route recorded accepted and which parses valid contributes its Done-when line with outcome CLARITY, BLESSED or SUCCESSOR and the digest; a failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdict covers nothing. An owner-confirmed or owner-overruled decision entry carrying that digest also covers; an unseen entry or retired reviewed key does not. claim_copy returns absent when its readable revision has no claimed specification for the work item, which releases every hold and produces neither a mint nor a brief; it returns unreadable when the revision or listed copy cannot be read, and unreadable_reason makes that a named hold at dispatch, hand close and merge and a mint or brief refusal. _copy_at gives both claim and current reads found, absent, or unreadable forms. blessing_owed owes when a readable current specification differs from a readable claim and has no cover; deleting its Done-when section reports every claimed item changed. An absent claim releases only for a row never claimed through that lane; an unreadable claim, unreadable current copy, or claimed row with no current active or closed specification holds naming why. spec_at returns path, text and why, and lane_hold compares a lane's claim at its base with its current or closed copy at the requested revision, and staged_closes identifies index moves from active into any terminal home: docs/archive/work/complete, partial or cancelled, or the legacy docs/work equivalent. For the commit being made, a staged entry for a claimed specification other than a lone addition is a change, and it is a close when the same staged change adds that specification in a terminal home; a lone addition is a new claim and is not listed. On the claim's own branch, only a close is judged against the lane's integration base; on the trunk, every listed change is judged against the commit's parent; elsewhere, only a close is judged against the current commit. An unreadable staged change set or branch identity holds naming the failed read, while outside a version-controlled work tree nothing is judged. The close listing returns an unreadable staged-change reason with its closes. Every path the staged-change reader classifies and every claim or current copy it looks up is read from a lossless, unquoted record listing, so a path that would otherwise be quoted is judged like any other. At the commit-time hold, each listed change is compared with HEAD, which is the commit's parent only for an ordinary commit. An amend is therefore compared with the commit it replaces; a cherry-pick, revert, rebase, or non-conflicting merge commit is written without the commit-time check; and a hand fast-forward creates no commit. A commit in a linked worktree on the integration branch while the primary checkout is on another branch is read as elsewhere, so a non-close change is not judged and a later close compares against the edited copy. Each named residue is caught after the fact only by the merge-time mint, and only when its before revision predates the change.\"","+code_symbol = \"bound_items/digest/covering/_copy_at/claim_copy/unreadable_reason/blessing/blessing_owed/hold_reason/spec_at/_wi_hold/lane_hold/classify_delta/_staged_delta/staged_changes/_relink_remap/_only_relinked/_staged_base/staged_hold\"","+detail = \"bound_items reads each current Done-when item that only ticks or evidences a claim-time item as that claim-time item; digest binds the work-item id and those items as sha256:<16 hex>. covering reads verdict lines under review records only through the one accepted-verdict reader: only a bound verdict whose route recorded accepted and which parses valid contributes its Done-when line with outcome CLARITY, BLESSED or SUCCESSOR and the digest; a failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdict covers nothing. An owner-confirmed or owner-overruled decision entry carrying that digest also covers; an unseen entry or retired reviewed key does not. claim_copy returns absent when its readable revision has no claimed specification for the work item, which releases every hold and produces neither a mint nor a brief; it returns unreadable when the revision or listed copy cannot be read, and unreadable_reason makes that a named hold at dispatch, hand close and merge and a mint or brief refusal. _copy_at gives both claim and current reads found, absent, or unreadable forms. blessing_owed owes when a readable current specification differs from a readable claim and has no cover; deleting its Done-when section reports every claimed item changed. An absent claim releases only for a row never claimed through that lane; an unreadable claim, unreadable current copy, or claimed row with no current active or closed specification holds naming why. spec_at returns path, text and why, and lane_hold compares a lane's claim at its base with its current or closed copy at the requested revision; the terminal homes are docs/archive/work/complete, partial or cancelled, or the legacy docs/work equivalent. For the commit being made, a staged entry for a claimed specification other than a lone addition is a change, and it is a close when the same staged change adds that specification in a terminal home; a lone addition is a new claim and is not listed. A claimed specification's lone modification is not a change when its staged bytes are exactly the inbound link redirect caused by another specification's move from active into a terminal home that the same staged change carries; any other modification, a real edit beside the redirect, an unreadable blob, or bytes that are not UTF-8 is a change. On the claim's own branch, only a close is judged against the lane's integration base; on the declared trunk, the branch the policy home names, in any worktree, every listed change is judged against the commit's parent; elsewhere, only a close is judged against the current commit. The trunk is read once whenever a claim-copy change is listed; an undeclared trunk holds every listed change naming the declaration, while a delta listing no claim-copy change never reads it. An unreadable staged change set or branch identity holds naming the failed read, while outside a version-controlled work tree nothing is judged. Every path the staged-change reader classifies and every claim or current copy it looks up is read from a lossless, unquoted record listing, so a path that would otherwise be quoted is judged like any other. At the commit-time hold, each listed change is compared with HEAD, which is the commit's parent only for an ordinary commit. The routes outside the supported commit routes and the two fail-closed shapes within the supported routes are stated once, as residue, in the shipped hook's `done-when-blessed` comment.\""," rationale = \"The same changed checklist is consulted at dispatch, hand close, merge and adjudication. One digest and covering predicate keep readers from accepting different texts or treating a verdict about an earlier text as a release.\""," test_refs = \"TC-325, TC-326\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3354,"oldLines":8,"newStart":3354,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-154\"]"," title = \"Single-plan pass gate\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/plan_gate.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/plan_pass.py\"","-code_symbol = \"_own_changes/_relinked/_close_homes/_regenerated/_is_home/build_paths/binding_problem/_unbound/record_problems/_reviewed_problems/_critique_problems/blocking_ids/_cap_problems/_dismissals/_record_lines/commit_lines/_parents/lane_rider/landing_refusal/_squash_lines/_claim_base/staged_lines/main/plan_dir/plan_path/record_path/critique_path/critique_round/bytes_digest/delta/parse/render/blob/plan_digest